  "assets": [
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.generating-function/learning_objective/1",
      "kind": "learning_objective",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.generating-function",
      "title": "Learning Objective",
      "content": "The student defines the probability generating function (PGF) G_X(z)=E[z^X]=∑_{k=0}^∞ P(X=k)z^k for non-negative integer-valued X; computes moments from the PGF via G^{(r)}(1)=E[X(X−1)⋯(X−r+1)]; defines the moment generating function (MGF) M_X(t)=E[e^{tX}] for any random variable; recovers moments via M^{(r)}(0)=E[X^r]; applies the key property that independence implies M_{X+Y}(t)=M_X(t)M_Y(t) (PGF: G_{X+Y}(z)=G_X(z)G_Y(z)); and uses PGFs to identify distributions via their generating function.",
      "localKey": null,
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.generating-function.md",
        "startLine": 18,
        "endLine": 21
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.generating-function/misconception/MC-1",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.generating-function",
      "title": "MGF-IS-JUST-A-TRICK-NOT-A-DISTRIBUTION-TOOL",
      "content": "| MC-1 | MGF-IS-JUST-A-TRICK-NOT-A-DISTRIBUTION-TOOL | Student treats the MGF as a computational shortcut but doesn't know it UNIQUELY determines the distribution: two random variables with the same MGF (wherever it exists) have identical distributions | Type 5 — instruction-induced (MGFs are introduced as \"moment machines\"; the uniqueness theorem is often mentioned briefly or omitted; students don't realise the MGF carries all distributional information, not just moments) |",
      "localKey": "MC-1",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-1"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.generating-function.md",
        "startLine": 28,
        "endLine": 28
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.generating-function/misconception/MC-2",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.generating-function",
      "title": "G-PRIME-AT-1-GIVES-E[X-SQUARED]",
      "content": "| MC-2 | G-PRIME-AT-1-GIVES-E[X-SQUARED] | Student differentiates G(z) once and evaluates at z=1 to get E[X²]; actually gets E[X(X−1)]=E[X²]−E[X]; doesn't apply the formula E[X²]=G''(1)+G'(1) to correct this | Type 1 — overgeneralisation (the first derivative evaluated at a CDF function gives a specific quantity; students apply the \"derivative at a point gives a moment\" heuristic without tracking the falling factorial vs. raw moment distinction) |",
      "localKey": "MC-2",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-2"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.generating-function.md",
        "startLine": 29,
        "endLine": 29
      }
    },
    {
      "assetId": "math.prob.generating-function/misconception/MC-3",
      "kind": "misconception",
      "conceptId": "math.prob.generating-function",
      "title": "INDEPENDENCE-MEANS-PGFS-ADD",
      "content": "| MC-3 | INDEPENDENCE-MEANS-PGFS-ADD | Student writes G_{X+Y}(z)=G_X(z)+G_Y(z) for independent X,Y instead of the correct product rule G_{X+Y}(z)=G_X(z)G_Y(z) | Type 1 — overgeneralisation (convolution of PMFs involves a sum — P_{X+Y}(k)=∑P_X(j)P_Y(k−j) — and students conflate \"summing probabilities\" with \"adding generating functions\"; the power-series multiplication is the correct mechanism) |",
      "localKey": "MC-3",
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [
        "MC-3"
      ],
      "span": {
        "file": "docs/curriculum/blueprints/math.prob.generating-function.md",
        "startLine": 30,
        "endLine": 30
      }
    },
    {
